Brute Force
-----------
Idea:
- Try every possible h from n down to 0.
- For each h, count how many papers have at least h citations.
- The first valid h is the answer.

Algorithm:
1. Let n = citations.size().
2. For h from n down to 0:
   - Count papers with citations >= h.
   - If count >= h, return h.
3. Return 0.

Time Complexity: O(n²)
Space Complexity: O(1)

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int hIndex(vector<int>& citations) {
        int n = citations.size();

        for (int h = n; h >= 0; h--) {
            int count = 0;

            for (int citation : citations) {
                if (citation >= h)
                    count++;
            }

            if (count >= h)
                return h;
        }

        return 0;
    }
};
```

Better Solution
---------------
Idea:
- Sort the citations in descending order.
- At index i, if citations[i] >= i + 1, then at least (i + 1) papers have that many citations.
- Keep updating the answer until the condition fails.

Algorithm:
1. Sort citations in descending order.
2. Traverse the array.
3. If citations[i] >= i + 1:
   - Update answer = i + 1.
4. Return answer.

Time Complexity: O(n log n)
Space Complexity: O(1)

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int hIndex(vector<int>& citations) {
        sort(citations.begin(), citations.end(), greater<int>());

        int h = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < citations.size(); i++) {
            if (citations[i] >= i + 1)
                h = i + 1;
            else
                break;
        }

        return h;
    }
};
```

Optimal Solution
----------------
Idea:
- The H-index can never exceed n (number of papers).
- Use counting sort (bucket sort).
- Count how many papers have exactly each citation count.
- Place all citations greater than n into the nth bucket.
- Traverse buckets from high to low while maintaining the cumulative number of papers.

Algorithm:
1. Create a bucket array of size n + 1.
2. For each citation:
   - If citation >= n, increment bucket[n].
   - Else increment bucket[citation].
3. Traverse buckets from n down to 0.
4. Maintain cumulative paper count.
5. Return the first i where cumulative count >= i.

Time Complexity: O(n)
Space Complexity: O(n)

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int hIndex(vector<int>& citations) {
        int n = citations.size();
        vector<int> bucket(n + 1, 0);

        for (int citation : citations) {
            if (citation >= n)
                bucket[n]++;
            else
                bucket[citation]++;
        }

        int papers = 0;

        for (int i = n; i >= 0; i--) {
            papers += bucket[i];

            if (papers >= i)
                return i;
        }

        return 0;
    }
};
```

Interview Notes
---------------
- Brute force checks every possible H-index and counts qualifying papers each time.
- Sorting makes it easy to determine how many papers have at least a given number of citations.
- The optimal bucket-counting solution works because the H-index can never be greater than the total number of papers.
- Why put citations greater than n into bucket[n]?
  - Any citation count larger than n behaves the same for H-index calculation because the maximum possible H-index is n.
- Example:
  - Citations = [3,0,6,1,5]
  - Sorted = [6,5,3,1,0]
  - The third paper has 3 citations, so H-index = 3.
- Edge Cases:
  - All citations are 0.
  - Single paper.
  - All papers have very high citation counts.
  - Duplicate citation counts.
- Follow-up:
  - What if the citations array is already sorted in ascending order?
    - Use binary search (LeetCode 275: H-Index II) to achieve O(log n).
- The bucket-counting approach is the expected optimal solution when the input is unsorted, while sorting is the most common interview solution.
